        "fragment": "for once := true; once || len(b) > 0; once = false {"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "close-during-transfer",
      "text": "With both ends open, if the reader closes before a pending Write hands off any bytes and no Read is ready, the Write returns zero and the reader's close error. If a Read accepts the slice first, it copies and acknowledges bytes even if Close runs before the acknowledgment. A fully acknowledged Write succeeds, while a partially acknowledged Write with no next Read ready returns its acknowledged count and the close error. If both calls have passed their initial checks and a channel match and closure are both selectable, the transfer can still win. Calls whose initial check observes the closed pipe return zero and a close error.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 88,
        "fragment": "nw := <-p.rdCh"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: How should the blocking and close contracts describe a transfer that remains selectable after done closes?"
    },
    {
      "id": "repeated-close",
      "text": "Starting with a fresh pipe, closing only the writer with nil makes later Reads return (0, EOF), and closing only the reader with nil makes later Writes return (0, ErrClosedPipe). When the first close on an end supplies a non-nil error, later operations on the still-open opposite end return that same error value. Further closes on the same end return nil and retain the first stored value, including a default stored by a nil close.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 24,
        "fragment": "if a.err != nil {"
